Input: {"question": "When did James Scudamore die?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d00", "start": 0, "end": 196, "text": "James Scudamore, 3rd Viscount Scudamore James Scudamore, 3rd Viscount Scudamore (1684 – 2 December 1716), was an English landowner and politician who sat in the House of Commons from 1705 to 1716."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d00", "start": 197, "end": 393, "text": "Scudamore was baptised on 15 July 1684, the second, but eldest surviving son of John Scudamore, 2nd Viscount Scudamore, and his wife Lady Frances Cecil, daughter of John Cecil, 4th Earl of Exeter."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d00", "start": 394, "end": 473, "text": "He matriculated at Gloucester Hall, Oxford in 1695 and was awarded DCL in 1712."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d00", "start": 474, "end": 569, "text": "He inherited the title Viscount Scudamore and the estate of Holme Lacy from his father in 1697."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d00", "start": 570, "end": 588, "text": "From 1698 to 1703,"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d01", "start": 0, "end": 547, "text": "history Tools Tools move to sidebar hide Actions Read Edit View history General What links here Related changes Upload file Permanent link Page information Cite this page Get shortened URL Download QR code Print/export Download as PDF Printable version In other projects Wikidata item Appearance move to sidebar hide From Wikipedia , the free encyclopedia English politician James Scudamore , 3rd Viscount Scudamore ( 1684 – 2 December 1716 ) , [ 1 ] was an English landowner and Tory politician who sat in the House of Commons from 1705 to 1716 ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d01", "start": 548, "end": 587, "text": "Holme Lacy Scudamore was baptised on 15"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d02", "start": 0, "end": 171, "text": "James Scudamore (died 1668) James Scudamore (26 June 1624 – 18 June 1668) was an English politician who sat in the House of Commons at various times between 1642 and 1668."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d02", "start": 172, "end": 362, "text": "Scudamore was the son of John Scudamore, 1st Viscount Scudamore of Holme Lacy, Herefordshire and his wife Elizabeth Porter, daughter of Sir Arthur Porter of Llanthony Abbey, Gloucestershire."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d02", "start": 363, "end": 394, "text": "He was baptised on 4 July 1624."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d02", "start": 395, "end": 504, "text": "His father was the ambassador to Paris from 1635 to 1639 and James Scudamore accompanied him there some time."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d02", "start": 505, "end": 595, "text": "Scudamore matriculated at St John's College, Oxford on 20 March 1640, aged 17 and remained"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d03", "start": 0, "end": 56, "text": "James ( 1624-68 ) , of Cary Cradock , Sellack , Herefs ."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d03", "start": 57, "end": 135, "text": "Published in The History of Parliament : the House of Commons 1660-1690 , ed ."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d03", "start": 136, "end": 141, "text": "B.D ."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d03", "start": 142, "end": 458, "text": "Henning , 1983 Available from Boydell and Brewer Biography Detail Constituency Family & Education Offices Held Biography End Notes Related Resources 1660-1690 Members 1660-1690 Constituencies 1660-1690 Parliaments 1660-1690 Surveys Previous Biography Next Biography Constituency Dates HEREFORD 26 July 1642 - 22 Jan."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d03", "start": 459, "end": 522, "text": "1644 HEREFORDSHIRE 1661 - 18 June 1668 Family and Education b ."}, "D4S6": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d03", "start": 523, "end": 544, "text": "26 June 1624 , 4th s."}, "D4S7": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d03", "start": 545, "end": 569, "text": "of John Scudamore † ( d."}, "D4S8": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d03", "start": 570, "end": 582, "text": "1671 ) , 1st"}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d04", "start": 0, "end": 7, "text": "26 Feb."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d04", "start": 8, "end": 84, "text": "1662 , and the bill passed the Lower House , but was rejected by the Lords ."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d04", "start": 85, "end": 137, "text": "However , the case inspired the Gaming Act of 1665 ."}, "D5S4": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d04", "start": 138, "end": 307, "text": "Scudamore was reluctant to act without ‘ Daddy ’ s advice ’ , but welcomed the second Dutch war as an opportunity to assuage his restlessness by serving with the fleet ."}, "D5S5": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d04", "start": 308, "end": 373, "text": "Nevertheless he was conscientious in attending quarter sessions ."}, "D5S6": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d04", "start": 374, "end": 457, "text": "He died in his father ’ s lifetime on 18 June 1668 , and was buried at Holme Lacy ."}, "D5S7": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d04", "start": 458, "end": 508, "text": "4 Ref Volumes : 1660-1690 Author : Edward Rowlands"}, "D6S1": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d05", "start": 0, "end": 160, "text": "James Scudamore (courtier) Sir James Scudamore (also spelled Skidmore, Skidmur or Skidmuer) (1568–1619) was a gentleman usher at the court of Queen Elizabeth I."}, "D6S2": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d05", "start": 161, "end": 359, "text": "Born at Holme Lacy, Herefordshire, he was the eldest son of John Scudamore, Custos Rotulorum of Herefordshire and his first wife Eleanor Croft, daughter of former Lord Deputy of Ireland James Croft."}, "D6S3": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d05", "start": 360, "end": 468, "text": "He would assume that title in 1616, and remained Custos Rotulorum for two years until his own death in 1619."}, "D6S4": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d05", "start": 469, "end": 560, "text": "Scudamore was respected for his tilting skill and his embodiment of the ideals of chivalry."}, "D6S5": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d05", "start": 561, "end": 611, "text": "Scudamore became something of a legend in his time"}, "D7S1": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0310-ec4d44d18b9b:d06", "start": 0, "end": 796, "text": "Member of the 1614 Parliament electoral district Herefordshire 1 reference stated in The History of Parliament History of Parliament ID 1604-1629/member/scudamore-sir-james-1568-1619 Commons category Sir James Scudamore 0 references Identifiers Early Modern Letters Online person ID 53727e2a-d64e-4571-9ec9-dfe0858a0640 0 references Freebase ID /m/05zxx73 0 references History of Parliament ID 1604-1629/member/scudamore-sir-james-1568-1619 0 references Oxford Dictionary of National Biography ID 76455 0 references Six Degrees of Francis Bacon ID 10010833 0 references genealogics.org person ID I00447542 0 references Geni.com profile ID 6000000001319680176 0 references The Peerage person ID p14527.htm # i145263 subject named as Sir James Scudamore 1 reference retrie